This is a simple message passing implementation for SMPs
--------------------------------------------------------

It is to be used by PARC when I get fed-up with MPICH/MP_Lite
as crap for debug.

It is based on the idea of the master instance (no -mype=<n>
command-line argument),  creating a matrix of FIFOs with
names like .fifo_<i>_<j>, to be used to send data from <i> to <j>, and
then spawning (npe-1) instances of
itself with the command line argument (-mype=<n>).

Registration consists of opening FIFOs for read and write as O_RDWR
which gets around (i think) the blocking conundrum (that if you try to
open a FIFO for O_RDONLY, then it blocks until someone opens it
for write, and vice-versa).

It is assumed that send-recv follows the SAME even-odd, odd-even
sequenced strategy, and it therefore should also not block.
This requires PARC to specify the actual PEs from which it wants
to receive data, not just the number (make the changes to parc.c).
DONE that.

The only hassle is likely to be FIFO buffer under-run:  the
reads must iterate until completion.  This is OK, since the
message lengths are ALWAYS known in advance (due to DUAL concept).

Finally, if we start the main instance with GDB, we can also
attempt to run the sinblings with GDB in separate (possibly
iconic) xterms.  This requires that GDB is driven by initial
"gdb.run.<n>" commands.  The main question is whether we can
pass command-line arguments in this way?????  NO, only if you
have version 5.1 of GDB or later (post RH7.2).  So we are back to
files!  Each sibling instance would require its own
personal gdb.run file:

   exec("xterm -e gdb --command=gdb.run.<n> program")

where "gdb.run.<n>" contains

   run <args from master instance, plus -mype=<n> >

That is OK, but begs the question of HOW the master instance
gets to find out how many PEs it is to run on?!!!??  I don't
want to do:

   xterm -e gdb --command=gdb.run.0 program

and put some crap into a fixed gdb.run.0;  that is why people
use mprun etc. to fire up parallel programs, even if they
don't execute the siblings there.  I would only need to do this
for debugging.  So an argument "-gdb" would tell registration
that it does not need to execute the siblings as a script will
do that.

WELL, for now the "smprun" script is rather hard-wired:  it is
run by

   smprun -npe=<n> program

It creates a reasonable number of

   gdb.run.<i>

files containing

   run -npe=<n> -mype=<i>

where the "-npe=<n>" argument is just assumed to be correct!  The
smp_register function always runs siblings in xterms with gdb
(why not?).  These sibling xterms are started iconic, so that we can
avoid clutter until necessary.

SO, does it work?  Well, the next step is to get hello to perform
the same kind of activity as a global send-recv, using the odd-even
rules.

OK, it seems to work:  i tried even sends to odd.  Getting about
 4Mw/s transfers between npe=4 for nw=10000.  That's cool.

ONE PROBLEM, i cannot seem to get sub-second clock timers to work.
ftime() appears to return weird numbers!  A combination of 
ftime (for ms) and time (for sec) is safe, but poor when
timer calls straddle a second boundary!

The smp_exit() function send SIGTERM to all of the "xterm" siblings
from master.  This means that normal termination will avoid
"killall gdb".
